Subject: the desktop keeps Ctrl + wheel when it holds enough icons to scroll

Ctrl + wheel on the desktop resizes the icons, through the containment
action bound to wheel:Vertical;ControlModifier. The Folder view hands
wheel events to the containment with a WheelInterceptor, and that
interceptor turned itself off as soon as the grid could scroll
(`!gridView.overflowing`). On a desktop with more icons than fit, the
grid therefore took every wheel event first, and Ctrl + wheel scrolled
instead of resizing: the gesture disappeared exactly on the desktops
busy enough to need it.

The interceptor now stays on and is told whether something below it
scrolls. A plain wheel is let through, so scrolling is untouched; a
wheel held with Ctrl goes to the containment, so the gesture holds.
Other modifiers keep the scrolling they have today, because widening
the exception to every modifier would quietly take Shift + wheel away
from the view below.

Its test comes with it, and so does what makes a Raye test run at all,
since this is the first one in the series. 🚨 That took three attempts,
because each failure looked like a pass. The packaging configures with
-DBUILD_TESTING=OFF and empties dh_auto_test, because one of the upstream
tests never ends, so a test added to an upstream autotests/ directory is
never run. Hence:

  * Raye's tests live in their own directory, gated on RAYE_TESTING,
    which the packaging turns on. Upstream's stay off, exactly as Ubuntu
    left them, and turning their directory on to reach one test would
    bring the one that never ends back with it.
  * enable_testing() is called by hand, because include(CTest) only calls
    it when BUILD_TESTING is on; without it add_test() registers nothing.
  * ctest runs with --no-tests=error, because it exits 0 when it finds
    nothing to run. That is how the first two attempts passed: the build
    said nothing, and there was nothing to say.
  * dh calls an override even under nocheck, so that skip is written out.

Reason: this cannot be done outside the source. The binding that turns
the interceptor off lives inside the containment's own QML package, and
no setting, script or look-and-feel package can reach it. Once it is
off, the wheel event never arrives at the containment at all, so no
containment action, plugin or shell script downstream has anything to
act on. The test has to run where the code is built, which is why the
switch that runs it belongs here too.

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -100,6 +100,18 @@
     include(ECMAddTests)
 endif()
 
+# Raye's own tests. The packaging configures with BUILD_TESTING=OFF, because
+# one of the upstream tests never ends, so upstream's stay off and Raye's get
+# a switch of their own that the packaging turns on.
+#
+# 🚨 enable_testing() is called by hand here: include(CTest) only calls it
+# when BUILD_TESTING is on. Without it add_test() registers nothing, and
+# ctest then reports that it found no test -- and exits 0. That is a build
+# that looks tested and is not.
+if(RAYE_TESTING)
+    enable_testing()
+endif()
+
 if(BUILD_DOC)
     find_package(KF6DocTools ${KF6_MIN_VERSION})
     set_package_properties(KF6DocTools PROPERTIES
